00:09:54.682 --> 00:09:57.140
RYAN GREGG: In fact, one of
the demos that we're doing here

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:01.790
at Next is using OpenOffice in
a container to convert a Word

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:03.170
document into a PDF.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:07.000
So we've taken, you know, old
legacy technology, embedded it

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.435
into the container, and then
we can run that serverlessly.

00:10:09.435 --> 00:10:10.588
It's pretty cool.

00:10:10.588 --> 00:10:12.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it sounds like there's

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:13.910
two different versions
of Cloud Run that

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.050
are kind of announced though.

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:16.530
What's the difference
between them?

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:17.572
RYAN GREGG: That's right.

00:10:17.572 --> 00:10:20.960
So there's Cloud Run, which
is the fully-managed, hosted

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:23.690
service for running
serverless containers.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.370
And there's Cloud
Run on GKE, which

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.960
provides many of
the same benefits,

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.300
but actually runs the compute
inside your Kubernetes

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:30.270
container.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.240
So you get the same
autoscaling benefits.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.205
You get the same
URL programming.

00:10:34.205 --> 00:10:36.830
You get the same container model
and the same container runtime

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:38.990
contract, but the compute
itself is actually

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:41.630
running inside your
Kubernetes Engine cluster

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.790
with the same
APIs, the same CLI,

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:45.980
the same Cloud Console
interface being

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:47.750
able to deploy
across both of them.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.220
So you get a great
story for being

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:51.350
able to use the platform
that makes the most

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.330
sense for your
organization, whether that's

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:56.420
a fully-managed hosted compute
product or the Kubernetes

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.540
product that you may
have already invested in.

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:00.957
GABRIELA FERRARA: For instance,
I already have, let's say,

00:11:00.957 --> 00:11:03.200
a GKE cluster that
I run some software.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:05.090
What kind of scenario
Cloud Run would

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:08.330
be useful for that to
run in there and not

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:09.438
in a managed service.

00:11:09.438 --> 00:11:11.480
RYAN GREGG: There's a
number of different reasons

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:12.813
why you might choose to do this.

00:11:12.813 --> 00:11:14.570
For one, you have a
lot more customization

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.090
in the types of compute
platform that you're running on.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:20.470
So if you need a bigger CPU
or more modern CPUs that

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:22.850
are available on Kubernetes,
you can have those.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:24.730
You can use high-memory
nodes in Kubernetes,

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.250
so you can have access to a
lot more RAM than what you'll

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.055
have on the hosted service.

00:11:29.055 --> 00:11:31.180
It also lets you run a
bunch of different workloads

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.990
together on the same
compute cluster.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.050
So if you have workloads
that you've previously

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.550
deployed on Kubernetes
that you want

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:39.730
to have low latency next to,
you can deploy those workloads

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:41.412
into your Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:41.412 --> 00:11:43.870
Kubernetes also gives you access
to hardware accelerators--

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:45.083
GPUs and TPUs.

00:11:45.083 --> 00:11:46.750
So if you have
serverless workloads that

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:49.420
want to take advantage of
that hardware acceleration,

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:52.000
you can deploy those from Cloud
Run into Kubernetes Engine

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.305
and access the GPUs as well.

00:11:53.305 --> 00:11:55.680
GABRIELA FERRARA: Machine
learning all the things, right?

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:56.560
RYAN GREGG: That's right.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So I mean, it's

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.210
fair to say that
if you were just

00:11:58.210 --> 00:11:59.680
kind of getting started
with it, you'd probably want

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.150
to use the full serverless one.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:01.900
RYAN GREGG: Absolutely, yeah.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.420
If you don't have
Kubernetes already

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.420
or you just want to kick
the tires of Cloud Run,

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.750
the hosted service is
absolutely the way to go.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.060
If you're a big organization
that has a big Kubernetes

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.910
investment, then using Cloud
Run on GKE probably makes sense.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:14.368
STEREN GIANNINI:
And the good thing

00:12:14.368 --> 00:12:16.360
is that it's super easy
to change your mind.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.050
We have customers who told
us, oh, I deployed first

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:22.300
on the fully-managed
version of Cloud Run

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.420
because I was looking for
an easy way to get started,

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:26.830
but then I realized
I was needing

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:30.400
to be close to my GKE workload,
so I moved it to my cluster.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.270
But from a developer
perspective,

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:33.790
they get the exact
same experience--

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:36.130
the exact same user interface,
the exact same command

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.502
line, the exact same API even.

00:12:38.502 --> 00:12:40.960
RYAN GREGG: This whole strategy
that we have with Cloud Run

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:43.300
is really based on what we've
heard from customers who

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:46.070
have been using App Engine,
Cloud Functions, and Kubernetes

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:49.420
Engine, where sometimes
for an IT [? dev ?] need

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.460
or for some particular
reason, they

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:54.420
want to move what they have
done from App Engine into GKE.

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.670
And in the past, that was
actually somewhat difficult.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:58.510
You had to think about how
to rebuild the application.

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:00.135
You had to learn how
Kubernetes worked.

00:13:00.135 --> 00:13:01.900
There was a lot
that went into it.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.730
Both Cloud Run and
Cloud Run on GKE, it's

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:04.910
actually just really simple.

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:06.868
You can just redeploy
the application container

00:13:06.868 --> 00:13:09.310
into your Kubernetes Engine
and everything works.

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:10.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Just take
it and chuck it over the wall.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:12.680
[DISHES BREAKING]
You're good to go.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:13.510
RYAN GREGG: Yep.

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:14.350
But you know,
workload portability

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:15.808
isn't the only
thing that we wanted

00:13:15.808 --> 00:13:17.110
to focus on with portability.

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:19.210
We wanted to make sure that
the skills that developers were

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.002
building up while they
were using Cloud Run

00:13:21.002 --> 00:13:23.020
were portable as well,
so that it wasn't just

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:24.978
that you were locked into
a serverless platform

00:13:24.978 --> 00:13:26.687
with the knowledge
that you were gaining,

00:13:26.687 --> 00:13:28.360
but you could deploy
it into Kubernetes

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.250
or through the open-source
Knative project

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:32.618
actually into any Kubernetes
cluster running anywhere,

00:13:32.618 --> 00:13:34.160
including an
on-premises environment.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:35.740
STEREN GIANNINI: I think we
have to give a little bit

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:36.970
more details here.

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.880
Cloud Run and Cloud
Run on GKE both

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.700
implement the
Knative Serving API,

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:44.560
which is an open-source
project we launched

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.170
last year at Cloud Next 2018.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:50.620
And Knative itself, you can
install it anywhere Kubernetes

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:51.250
run.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.750
So if you think
about it, that means

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.750
that the tooling that
you've built around this API

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:58.780
is not only compatible with
Cloud Run, Cloud Run on GKE,

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.650
but also with any
Knative implementation.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.840
And we have many partners who
are authoring managed Knative

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:07.550
in their cloud platforms.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:08.440
RYAN GREGG: Yep.

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:09.982
GABRIELA FERRARA:
That's how you keep

00:14:09.982 --> 00:14:12.274
the scale to zero on your
cluster with Knative helping.

00:14:12.274 --> 00:14:13.565
RYAN GREGG: Yeah, that's right.

00:14:13.565 --> 00:14:15.550
One of the features that
Knative Serving offers

00:14:15.550 --> 00:14:18.670
is the ability to scale to zero
inside the Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.760
So we don't change the way the
Kubernetes cluster is set up.

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.140
We don't change the provisioned
nodes in the cluster.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:27.130
But we can scale the instances
of a container down to zero

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:28.750
or if you're familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:31.175
the pods in that cluster
down to zero for workload.

00:14:31.175 --> 00:14:32.800
And there's another
really cool feature

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:35.217
that I think came out this
week, which is called GKE usage

00:14:35.217 --> 00:14:37.690
metering, which lets
you, as an organization,

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:39.430
see how people are
using the resources

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.200
inside your Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:44.020
And so by combining Cloud Run
on GKE or Knative with that,

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:46.270
you can actually do
a scale-to-zero cost

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.880
for your internal teams when
how you charge them back

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.020
for the resources they're
using in your cluster.

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:52.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
you've got this ability

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:54.820
now to take these containers
fully-portable move them

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:57.038
around, whether it's
Kubernetes hosted with Google,

00:14:57.038 --> 00:14:59.080
Kubernetes hosted somewhere
else, you can kind of

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:00.400
move all these things around.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:01.780
What's the best way
that someone's actually

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.970
going to start, like,
managing their build pipelines

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:04.777
with this?

00:15:04.777 --> 00:15:06.610
STEREN GIANNINI: So of
course, everybody who

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:08.470
thinks container thinks Docker.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:11.360
And Docker is an amazing
tool to build and push

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:12.340
your Docker container.

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:15.940
There is a very large
ecosystem around Dockerfile.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:17.625
If you don't know
what a Dockerfile is,

00:15:17.625 --> 00:15:19.000
it's the very
small file that you

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.570
put to describe how to build
your code into a container.

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:24.820
And don't be afraid if
you don't know them.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:25.910
Take a look at them.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.000
They are just a very
few lines of code

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.920
where you start
from a base image.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.950
There are many official base
image-- like each language

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:36.472
community has official base
images posted on Docker Hub.

00:15:36.472 --> 00:15:37.930
And you know, you
basically declare

00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.310
what you want to install
into your container.

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:42.430
So Dockerfiles
and Docker overall

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:45.760
is an industry standard
today, but Google Cloud

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:49.030
provides ways to build your
containers in the cloud itself,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:52.130
instead of your local
machines or on-premise.

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:54.610
So this product is
called Cloud Build.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:57.880
So Cloud Build will take your
source and your Dockerfiles

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:00.640
and package this up
into a container image,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.290
fully on demand.

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:04.330
So I consider Cloud
Build serverless.

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:05.770
You don't have to
see any servers.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:07.320
You pay only when you build.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.220
That qualifies as
serverless to me.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.830
And so Cloud Build and Cloud
Run are a good match, right?

00:16:12.830 --> 00:16:16.060
You go from your code
to build artifacts

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:18.430
that you build with
Cloud Build, and then you

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.060
deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.250
What's also super interesting
is that Cloud Build

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:24.290
has Cloud Build triggers.

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:26.060
What that means is
that I can decide

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:30.080
to trigger a build when I'm
pushing some commits into a Git

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:30.920
branch.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:32.960
So now, we start doing
continuous integration

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:36.520
and continuous delivery because
every time the repo is updated,

00:16:36.520 --> 00:16:39.590
Cloud Build will build a
container, and can also as you

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.710
want, but can also
deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.140
RYAN GREGG: I also add Cloud
Build is a phenomenal tool

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:47.740
for doing builds and doing
a lightweight CI, like what

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:48.678
Steren just described.

00:16:48.678 --> 00:16:50.720
But the great thing about
using Docker containers

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.120
as the primitive for
deploying compute here

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:55.280
is that you can actually
use any CI/CD system

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:56.540
that you have already.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.252
One of our launch
partners, GitLab,

00:16:58.252 --> 00:17:00.710
is actually showing off some
of their serverless technology

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:04.069
here as well, where you can use
GitLab to deploy serverlessly

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.290
into Cloud Run or to Knative.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:09.530
So you can use Cloud Build if
you're starting from scratch

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:12.800
or you can use your
existing CI/CD pipelines

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:15.087
to flow the container
straight into Cloud Run.

00:17:15.087 --> 00:17:16.670
STEREN GIANNINI:
Last week, buildpacks

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.700
released a beta version
of the pack command line.

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:22.640
And buildpacks allow you
to go from source code

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:26.680
in various languages
directly to containers

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:28.858
without having to think
about Dockerfiles even.

00:17:28.858 --> 00:17:30.650
I really recommend to
check out buildpacks.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:32.720
It's a great match
to build containers

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.617
when you are a language expert.

00:17:34.617 --> 00:17:36.950
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yeah, I
have trouble with Dockerfiles.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:41.040
I always have to fight them
or I'd lose it and in the end,

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:42.450
someone helps me.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.783
RYAN GREGG: Yeah, the great
thing about the pack command

00:17:44.783 --> 00:17:47.000
line tool that just got
launched is that you can just

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:49.640
type one command inside
a folder with source,

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:52.340
and it leverages the buildpacks
to go ahead and create a Docker

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:53.420
container for you.

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.587
STEREN GIANNINI: Oh, so if
you are a Java developer,

00:17:55.587 --> 00:17:57.230
I heard that JIB--

00:17:57.230 --> 00:17:59.480
Java Image Builder--
was an amazing tool.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:03.170
You just import a Maven
package in your pom.xml,

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:06.230
and then in one command,
you can build your Java app

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:07.190
into a container.

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:09.645
You don't even need
Docker installed locally.

00:18:09.645 --> 00:18:11.270
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yeah, the Jib thing--

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:12.290
it was a couple weeks ago.

00:18:12.290 --> 00:18:13.100
I think it was pretty recent.

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:13.610
It just came out.

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:14.040
And you can easily--

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.190
I think you said, Maven
or Gradle, put it in your

00:18:16.190 --> 00:18:18.260
build pipeline, and then,
bam, in the same way,

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:19.490
you're building your
containers without having

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:20.810
to worry about all
the little tweaks

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:22.530
that you might need to
put in the Dockerfile.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:23.240
RYAN GREGG: This is one
of the reasons we're

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:24.940
really excited about
having containers

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.565
as the primitive is because, you
know, there is so much tooling

00:18:27.565 --> 00:18:30.950
and such a large ecosystem built
up around Docker containers

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.770
that you can use whatever
tools you want to be

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:35.920
able to deploy onto Cloud Run.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:38.420
GABRIELA FERRARA: I never could
get past the Docker Compose.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:40.410
Everybody said that
was easier to use,

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.280
but, like, I sticked with
the Dockerfile itself.

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:44.385
I'm going to check back.

00:18:44.385 --> 00:18:46.510
STEREN GIANNINI: I would
recommend in the Cloud Run

00:18:46.510 --> 00:18:49.730
Quickstart, we are giving
very simple Dockerfiles

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:52.500
in popular languages for
people to get started.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.890
So if you're a Node.js
developer, don't be scared.

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:56.960
The Dockerfile is
literally five lines

00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:00.410
from Node.js, npm
install, and npm start.

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.160
That's what it says.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.370
GABRIELA FERRARA: The Cloud
Run was in alpha, right?

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.028
And now, it's in beta, correct?

00:19:06.028 --> 00:19:07.070
STEREN GIANNINI: Correct.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:07.550
RYAN GREGG: That's right.

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.050
GABRIELA FERRARA:
So what are people

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:10.680
are building on it usually?

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.700
STEREN GIANNINI: So we've
seen two major categories

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:14.880
of use cases.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:18.050
The first one would be
exposing public services

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:19.070
to the internet.

00:19:19.070 --> 00:19:21.920
So a lot of them have been
using Cloud Run to just build

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:25.580
websites, or APIs,
or mobile back-ends.

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:27.590
Some did chatbots,
where you expose

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:30.110
just a simple endpoint
that will reply

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.060
every time the request comes in.

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:34.520
This is all about
public microservices.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:36.050
So the great thing
about Cloud Run

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.910
is that it will
automatically scale

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:39.540
based on incoming requests.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:43.070
So if your website or API
is popular, no problem.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:43.880
Cloud Run scales.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.340
And if it's not,
well, no problem

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:47.215
either because Cloud
Run scales down to zero.

00:19:47.215 --> 00:19:48.965
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Hopefully, it's popular.

00:19:48.965 --> 00:19:50.630
STEREN GIANNINI:
Hopefully-- we hope.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:53.990
But we've also seen a
large amount of use cases

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:56.670
around private microservices.

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:58.790
So this would be
people who are using

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:01.790
Cloud Run to do data
transformation or image

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:04.580
analysis because the
interesting thing is that Cloud

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:06.770
Run integrates with Pub/Sub.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:11.510
So from Pub/Sub, you can push
an event to a Cloud Run service

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:14.240
entirely privately without
having to expose anything

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.180
to the internet.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:18.710
And Cloud Run has a request
timeout of 15 minutes.

00:20:18.710 --> 00:20:22.220
That means that for 15 minutes,
you can process an image,

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:24.920
convert a DOC to a PDF
like we are doing today

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:26.120
during the demo.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:29.180
So for example, we have
Airbus that you probably all

00:20:29.180 --> 00:20:32.550
know-- they are using Cloud
Run to run inside a container

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.930
one of their custom binaries to
do image detection on satellite

00:20:35.930 --> 00:20:38.610
images, completely
in a serverless way.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.610
When they don't use it,
they don't pay anything.

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:41.450
It scales to zero.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:43.880
And when they have a lot
of images to process,

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:44.990
well, it scales.

00:20:44.990 --> 00:20:47.715
GABRIELA FERRARA: So 50 minutes
is the timeout you said?

00:20:47.715 --> 00:20:48.590
STEREN GIANNINI: 15--

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:48.820
1-5.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:49.420
GABRIELA FERRARA: 15, OK.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:20:50.920
STEREN GIANNINI:
Yeah, on Cloud Run.

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.598
Cloud Run on GKE can do more.

00:20:52.598 --> 00:20:54.890
RYAN GREGG: Yeah, we have a
little bit more flexibility

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:58.700
on the Kubernetes side to
make the timeout be longer.

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:01.200
But of course, it's still
based on the length of an HTTP

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:03.770
request, so you know, there's
some network stability issues

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:06.350
that you may encounter if
you try to push it too much.

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:07.910
GABRIELA FERRARA: I'm asking
because on Functions, it's

00:21:07.910 --> 00:21:09.180
5 minutes if I recall--

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.200
6 minutes if I recall.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:10.910
STEREN GIANNINI: I
think you can push it

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:11.910
to 9 on Cloud Functions.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:13.410
GABRIELA FERRARA:
Last time used it,

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:14.930
and I was trying
to do an ETL on it.

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:17.040
And that was not a good idea.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:19.040
RYAN GREGG: Cloud Run
will be a much better tool

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:22.212
for doing things like ETL for
real data processing work.

00:21:22.212 --> 00:21:24.170
GABRIELA FERRARA: Oh, I
need to check that out.

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:25.878
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, speaking of which

00:21:25.878 --> 00:21:27.530
though, so now,
we've got Cloud Run.

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:28.950
We've got Cloud Functions.

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:30.350
And of course, we were just
talking about App Engine

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:31.042
earlier.

00:21:31.042 --> 00:21:32.750
There's a lot of
different solutions here

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:33.930
that cover a lot of space.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:35.480
We know that, you know, you
just kind of want a container.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:36.260
You want to throw it up there.

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.370
Cloud Run sounds like
the best way to do it.

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.670
But if someone's getting
started with all of this

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:43.280
and someone says, oh, you
should go serverless, great.

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:45.860
But now, I've got at least three
different compute serverless

00:21:45.860 --> 00:21:47.270
options to choose from.

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:49.670
What's kind of the best way
to guide them down that path?

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:50.780
Is there a decision tree?

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:52.447
Is there, like, a
couple of quick fixes?

00:21:52.447 --> 00:21:53.880
What would make sense there?

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:55.380
STEREN GIANNINI:
So to start with, I

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.260
would say you have
to ask yourself

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:00.230
which level of abstraction
do I want to be at?

00:22:00.230 --> 00:22:03.920
If you really want to attach
some very small snippet of code

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:07.280
next to your database, Cloud
Functions will be ideal.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:10.820
You will just provide a
function to do one thing,

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:12.380
and it will scale on demand.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:13.640
It builds for you.

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:15.590
You don't have to
deal with containers.

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:17.950
Functions have a
lot of event sources

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:21.080
that are not available in
Cloud Run or App Engine.

00:22:21.080 --> 00:22:24.260
So you can also use
Function to do HTTP,

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:28.010
but more importantly,
event-triggered functions.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:31.190
App Engine, I think, is
ideal for web workloads.

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:33.740
App Engine has been
designed for the web API,

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:36.260
or the website use
case, or maybe the web

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:37.500
back-end use case.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.990
So App Engine is
source based, so it

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:44.750
will support now five or
seven languages very well.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:47.660
And you know, you go from
your local source code,

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:50.390
you hit one command, and
boom, the thing is deployed.

00:22:50.390 --> 00:22:53.500
You don't see the
containers on App Engine.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.120
On Cloud Run, I would
say it's anything

00:22:56.120 --> 00:22:58.400
that fits the container
contract of Cloud Run.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.955
So here, your
imagination is the limit.

00:23:00.955 --> 00:23:02.330
The contract is
that we are going

00:23:02.330 --> 00:23:05.390
to send you events or
requests on an endpoint

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:08.090
to your container, and
then you handle the rest.

00:23:08.090 --> 00:23:10.735
So Cloud Run would be for
anything else, I would say.

00:23:10.735 --> 00:23:12.770
But the good news is
that the three of them

00:23:12.770 --> 00:23:15.260
are built on the
same Google Cloud

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:16.550
serverless infrastructure.

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:19.450
They all share the same
sandbox technology,

00:23:19.450 --> 00:23:22.610
allowing [INAUDIBLE] Google
to run unmodified languages

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:23.750
in the stack.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:26.150
And maybe one last thing
is that Cloud Functions

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.420
is generally available, App
Engine is generally available.

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:34.090
Cloud Run literally launched
45 minutes ago to beta.

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:36.608
So depending on your
taste for risk--

00:23:36.608 --> 00:23:38.900
RYAN GREGG: If you're trying
to go to production today,

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:41.210
I think App Engine or Cloud
Functions is definitely

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.360
the right choice for now.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:43.100
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
it's a great option

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:45.590
for people who want to get
started, maybe check it out.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:46.495
STEREN GIANNINI:
And they all-- you

00:23:46.495 --> 00:23:47.870
know, App Engine,
Cloud Functions

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:49.340
are fully-supported products.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:53.030
We invested a lot into App
Engine in the past years

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:54.300
and keep investing.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:57.620
We launched many new languages
on second-generation runtimes

00:23:57.620 --> 00:23:59.510
on App Engine
standard environment,

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:00.850
and we keep improving.

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:03.470
At Cloud Next 2019, we
are announcing the ability

00:24:03.470 --> 00:24:06.350
to connect to VPC networks
from App Engine, which

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:09.690
was a huge demand
from our customers.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:12.112
So we are heavily investing
in those three products.

00:24:12.112 --> 00:24:13.820
GABRIELA FERRARA:
That's very interesting

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:16.020
because on App
Engine, you couldn't

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.180
connect to the private network.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:19.950
So if you wanted
to have something

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.410
that's a private process--

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:23.060
like, let's say, a
Kubernetes cluster

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:24.560
that you didn't put it outside--

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:27.140
and you have something
processing there called run,

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:28.640
you can use it now.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:30.200
RYAN GREGG: Yeah, it used to be
that everything had to go over

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:32.300
the public internet to be
able to talk between App

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:33.420
Engine and something else.

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:36.260
But now with VPC
connect, you can actually

00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:38.840
use the VPC to do all of
that internal network.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:39.590
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Anything else really

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.548
cool that you've seen at
Next that you're super

00:24:41.548 --> 00:24:42.740
excited about?

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:45.260
STEREN GIANNINI: We are super
excited about introducing

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:46.310
Cloud Run to the world.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:49.490
We've been holding the
announcement for so long now.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:52.700
We've been interacting with
thousands of alpha testers

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.560
who gave us tremendous feedback.

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.560
If they are listening, I really
want to thank them for that.

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:58.940
They helped us
shape the product.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.710
They helped us fix issues.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:04.040
And now, we are really very
excited to give it to the world

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:07.250
and see what developers
will build with Cloud Run

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.920
because, you know, arbitrary
stateless containers

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:11.640
on a fully-serverless
environment--

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:13.898
I think the imagination
